Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:08.950 align:start position:0%
 
this morning on the dish  arizona-born indigenous chef brian  yazzie he comes from the navajo nation  where he learned ancient food traditions  from his family  now he's brought them to minnesota using  local ingredients to create modern  dishes while  caring for his community in a particular  time of need  jamie yukis has his story this is  dehydrated prairie sage the ingredients  techniques and presentation are all  rooted in tradition  you almost made like a squash dip yeah  pretty much  did your mom make indigenous food  growing up i remember my mom  stepping out the back door getting some  lambs quarters known as wild spinach she  would pull down to the potato super  mutton  monster you know just a little taste of  the landscape that was my  introduction to native food indigenous  food is known for three  key ingredients called the three sisters  it's corn bean squash  they definitely help each other grow in  the garden and they also complement each  other you know  on the table as well so but corn is one  of the main staples with with my tribe  and here in the midwest there's one  sister that we add called the fourth  sister and that is wild rice  brian yazzie started cooking for his  family a big family  when he was just seven years old being  the youngest out of eight sibling  and coming from a single pound household  i tried  my best growing up to help my mom when  he wasn't behind the stove  yazzie got involved with the wrong crowd  in arizona i started following the  negativity in the environment  gang activity stealing car stereos so i  did not receive my high school diploma  until  i was at the age of 25. then one day  while out on the streets  came a call from his sister that led him  to his passion  she was saying we missed her cooking why  don't you come home and  prepare a meal for the week i would come  home for days at a time to cook  and that was their way of checking on me  you know so i came off the term that  cook can save my life  but it wasn't until his now wife huna  mana gave him an  ultimatum moved to minnesota together  for a fresh start  or she was leaving him that's when he  knew he needed to choose a new path  and when they received the brochure in  the mail from saint paul college  and i see all the shiny appliances you  know the fruits the bed the flames so  that hooked me  now his goal is to inspire the next  generation of young chefs  my philosophy is cooking into worlds  ancestral knowledge with modern  techniques traveling around the world  sharing his knowledge and picking up  local native ingredients  used in dishes he prepares at the  gathering cafe it's almost like a meal  now  like this hot chocolate yazzie's  assistant vanessa casillas stirred up  for us  i cannot wait to try this yeah cheers  oh my gosh it smells wonderful so this  is the soup  yeah this one is red chili pizzoli and  it has a roasted duck  and the corn is a traditional corn to  the navajo tribe  what's a little bit of spice in here um  it would be red chili new mexican  red chili minnesotans in general are not  known for  liking spicy or anything no no too crazy  no i had to balance out you know um i  started with ketchup  no i'm kidding no it had to bounce out  more mild  more um you know more of a smoky flavor  what i tasted earlier that had the spice  in at the pizzool  yeah would you normally have that like  two times as hot 10 times as hot  probably five times it's hot yeah really  yeah yeah i try not to do too much when  i'm cooking  regardless of manipulating one  ingredient i try and keep it with his  original flavor profile  no especially with the owlers i try and  reintroduce these type of ingredients  that i've been forgotten about  reintroducing these type of natural food  products  is kind of it's more of a struggle when  the pandemic hit the cafe was forced to  close  and his team went from serving a hundred  meals a week in its dining room  to cooking and delivering meals to  native elders every day for  free grants local health care companies  and jose andreas charity world central  kitchen  are helping fund the meals i think a lot  of us who pay attention to news  headlines have been just so  concerned about the navajo nation when  it comes to the pandemic and how hard  it's been hit  here in twin cities it's the top five  urban native community  in north america majority of the elders  are the last people holding on to the  languages and the cultures so that was  my way of  giving back to the community if the  elders die and the language dies  yeah what do you have anymore culturally  yeah i mean the same thing with food  right  has it been interesting to you that it's  become almost a fad to have indigenous  food or people wanting to hear more from  indigenous  chefs yeah i mean i think the time is  now five years ago you wouldn't have  heard about this  probably heard about indian taco as a  fry bread and so now you have people  talking about heirloom squash  different type of amaranth or quinoa we  have always been here you know we're now  we're just circling back to  reclaiming our spaces in the kitchen and  how lucky for all of us who get to  experience the culture  and learn about the past through yazi's  cooking  for cbs this morning saturday jamie  yukas minneapolis  i miss us tasting  [Music]  i know and the colors of it all just  using all of those things from the past  and sort of reintroducing them in  different ways like that traditional and  the modern  is one of my favorite things to see with  chefs how great did that hot chocolate  look  right this morning we're hungry

